IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Interpretation Act 1984 (Western Australia) -- section 5, the terms the Act defines (106 entries, one per defined term line).
-- Section 5 says: in this Act and every other written law, the words below mean what is said here.  Each entry is a table row:
-- the words defined, how they are defined (means, includes, both, or the meaning is given by another provision), where that
-- other provision is, and the things the definition lists.  Meanings that are rules of reading in their own right are encoded
-- in the module for their Part (section 62 months, section 13A de facto partners, and so on); the entry here points to them.
-- The text of each definition is quoted above its entry.

§ `Section 5 -- Terms used in written laws`

DECLARE `Way a term is defined` IS ONE OF
    `it means`
    `it includes`
    `it means and includes`
    `its meaning is given elsewhere`

DECLARE `Defined term` HAS
    `the words defined`          IS A LIST OF STRING
    `how it is defined`          IS A `Way a term is defined`
    `where its meaning is given` IS A MAYBE STRING
    `what the definition lists`  IS A LIST OF STRING
    `the list is complete`       IS A BOOLEAN


-- What an entry's list holds.  For a term defined by `includes`, the things the section says it includes.  For a term defined by
-- `means`, the meaning as printed (the words after "means", up to the semicolon) and, where the printed meaning is itself a list or a
-- pair of alternatives ("A or B"), each constituent as well; the same for the `means` limb of a `means and includes` term.  A
-- constituent is a slice of the printed line, or is composed from slices by distributing a shared frame over a printed list
-- ("the exercise of a" over "power, responsibility, authority or jurisdiction").  `the list is complete` is TRUE only where the
-- list names every thing the term covers (a `means` over named things); a descriptive meaning ("a natural person") is stored as its
-- words, and a thing that is not those words is NOTHING, not FALSE, because a failed string match does not show that a differently
-- worded thing is outside the description.  Lookup is by exact string; no article, plural or case is normalised.

-- src:128 | act used with reference to an offence or civil wrong includes an omission and extends to a series of acts or omissions or a series of acts and omissions;
@ref s 5 "act" (src:128)
GIVETH A `Defined term`
`section 5 term: act` MEANS
    `Defined term` WITH
        `the words defined`          IS LIST "act"
        `how it is defined`          IS `it includes`
        `where its meaning is given` IS NOTHING
        `what the definition lists`  IS LIST "an omission", "a series of acts or omissions", "a series of acts and omissions"
        `the list is complete`       IS FALSE

-- src:129 | Act means any Act or Ordinance passed by the Parliament of Western Australia, or by any Council previously having authority or power to pass laws in Western Australia, such Act or Ordinance having been assented to by or on behalf of Her Majesty;
@ref s 5 "Act" (src:129)
GIVETH A `Defined term`
`section 5 term: Act (line 129)` MEANS
    `Defined term` WITH
        `the words defined`          IS LIST "Act"
        `how it is defined`          IS `it means`
        `where its meaning is given` IS NOTHING
        `what the definition lists`  IS LIST "any Act or Ordinance passed by the Parliament of Western Australia, or by any Council previously having authority or power to pass laws in Western Australia, such Act or Ordinance having been assented to by or on behalf of Her Majesty"
        `the list is complete`       IS FALSE

